1
00:00:07,330 --> 00:00:11,288
I booked this two weeks ago!

2
00:00:11,810 --> 00:00:12,890
Sorry.

3
00:00:12,890 --> 00:00:15,768
We have no cars.

4
00:00:16,290 --> 00:00:22,288
Rental companies plan around expected returns, not a specific car waiting for you.

5
00:00:22,288 --> 00:00:29,457
One late return, one car in the shop, one at the wrong branch—and three expected cars become zero.

6
00:00:34,466 --> 00:00:39,133
Then upgrade me, or downgrade me to whatever car you have.

7
00:00:39,650 --> 00:00:41,843
I'm sorry, neither is possible.

8
00:00:41,843 --> 00:00:43,046
They're all gone.

9
00:00:43,046 --> 00:00:46,442
If you wait two hours, you may get compensation.

10
00:01:01,090 --> 00:01:05,048
That's an H car.

11
00:01:05,570 --> 00:01:10,785
I rent this car from H through their rideshare driver program.

12
00:01:10,785 --> 00:01:14,487
A car sitting in the lot earns them nothing.

13
00:01:15,010 --> 00:01:18,968
So what happens when she gets too old?

14
00:01:19,490 --> 00:01:22,796
She had two hundred miles when I got her.

15
00:01:22,796 --> 00:01:26,990
Soon H sells her, while she's still worth something.

16
00:01:31,970 --> 00:01:32,827
Oh, true.

17
00:01:32,827 --> 00:01:36,637
H sells lots of its cars on H Car Sales.

18
00:01:52,706 --> 00:01:58,789
Safe travels!

19
00:01:59,298 --> 00:02:05,481
Next time, add your flight number so the rental desk can track delays.

20
00:02:05,481 --> 00:02:10,340
Check the pickup terms, and keep a backup ride in mind.

